00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.480
Abraham: So, what exactly is
social engineering and why do

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.260
cyber criminals like to use it?

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.510
Social engineering is the name given
to techniques used by cyber criminals

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:13.340
to manipulate and trick people into
giving out confidential information,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.160
transferring money or downloading malware.

00:00:16.609 --> 00:00:19.610
Nicole: Social engineers use
psychology to manipulate a

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.890
victim’s feelings of curiosity
or fear to draw them into a trap.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.689
They often find it is easier to exploit
a person’s willingness to trust rather

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:32.030
than go to the trouble of trying to
discover ways to hack their software.

00:00:32.639 --> 00:00:37.160
As technology and subsequently cyber
security gets more advanced, cyber

00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:42.090
criminals are now turning more to social
engineering, to make their initial attack

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.400
on a system by exploiting the weakest
link in an organisational chain the user.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.300
Abraham: Unlike cyber attacks that
rely on system vulnerabilities to gain

00:00:51.349 --> 00:00:56.510
access to unauthorised devices, social
engineering targets human vulnerabilities.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.150
They can use a variety of methods
both online and offline such as phone

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:06.649
calls, emails, text messages or other
means to con unsuspecting users into

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.680
compromising their own systems or
their work-based systems by installing

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.445
malware and handing over access to their
sensitive and personal information and

00:01:15.445 --> 00:01:17.469
potentially that of their employer.

00:01:18.289 --> 00:01:32.160
Nicole: We have already looked at
OSINT or open source intelligence

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:32.170
information in previous videos but
let’s have a quick recap and look at

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:32.183
how open source information can help
cyber criminals with social engineering.

00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:34.360
Abraham: Open-source information
is information that is publicly

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.199
available, and anyone can obtain by
request, purchase or observation.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.770
Information to be considered open source
can be published or broadcast for a

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.659
public audience, available to the public
by request, subscription or purchase.

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:52.960
It can also be seen or heard
casually or made available at a

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.429
public meeting or by visiting a
place or event open to the public.

00:01:58.679 --> 00:02:02.570
Nicole: Cyber criminals can use
open-source intelligence to identify

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.140
weaknesses to exploit in networks such
as accidental leaks of information

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:12.290
through social media, open ports or
unsecured internet connected devices.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.880
Cyber criminals can also access
publicly available information

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.500
through social media sites.

00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:23.479
Just think about all that information
people put online, ages, addresses,

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:28.190
email addresses, family and pet names,
what they are doing, where they go, what

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.320
they believe in, what they have bought.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.429
The list is endless and gives plenty
of information to cyber criminals.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.650
That's just a small portion of
the information that is available.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.350
Abraham: Think about all the
other information that can

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.650
be obtained via the internet.

00:02:42.199 --> 00:02:45.510
So how do cyber criminals use the
information they have obtained

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.200
by open source intelligence
and what do they do with it?

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:53.639
Nicole: Social engineering is a way
of manipulating people into breaking

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:55.329
their normal security protocols.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.600
The cyber criminal uses these tactics to
exploit an individual’s trust so that they

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.010
can hack into their systems and software.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.880
Abraham: So how can they do this
and how does this affect us?

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.690
Nicole: By using OSINT, cyber criminals
can make their attacks look legitimate,

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.190
they do this by lowering your defences.

00:03:14.509 --> 00:03:18.829
They check out your details on social
media and have a back story prepared.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.920
Abraham: Cyber criminals will build
an image of who they are pretending

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.640
to be, so they can talk confidently
about it and make you feel for them

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.519
to increase your sense of familiarity.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.019
They make you more likely to do what
they want, such as click a link.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.019
This is called pretexting.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:38.450
They may, for example, research Instagram
and see that you have a pet dog and when

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.970
they make contact, could talk about how
they’ve been working at the dog shelter.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.390
This will build an emotional
connection and establish themselves

00:03:45.429 --> 00:03:47.140
as a good and trustworthy person.

00:03:48.139 --> 00:03:51.839
Nicole: So what useful information can
be gained through social engineering?

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:56.480
Cybercriminals through social
engineering use multiple tactics to

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.579
gain information such as passwords,
usernames and banking details.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.350
They create websites which look
legitimate using trademarks and logos.

00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:09.569
They rely on an individual’s
trust that it must be 'legit'.

00:04:10.369 --> 00:04:14.650
By accessing one person’s email
password they also have access to

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.079
all their contacts and many people
use the same password so they may

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:23.040
have access to their social media
and all their social media contacts.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.159
Think of all those emails about winning
money or prizes, be eligible for a tax

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.480
refund, helping someone out abroad by
accepting their money into your account.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.840
They are all ways social
engineering takes place so just

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.560
think have you ever been a victim.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.530
Abraham: As well as traditional
social engineering where the

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.049
attacker contacts the victim.

00:04:44.429 --> 00:04:47.759
There is also another tactic
called reverse social engineering.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.880
In reverse social engineering the cyber
criminal will use OSINT to manipulate

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.610
a victim into contacting them.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.060
Nicole: This could first start with
a phishing link which invites the

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.729
victim to respond, maybe they want
to upgrade their energy supply,

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:03.400
or have a parcel redelivered.

00:05:03.849 --> 00:05:07.240
If they click on the link and put
in their details, they are hooked.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.970
Abraham: The attackers also use
‘domain squatting’ which involves

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.830
setting up a fake website that
resemble one the victim uses regularly

00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:16.779
but with a slightly different name.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.489
They wait for the victim to
misspell the web address and click

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.740
on the familiar looking link and
add their username and password.

00:05:24.129 --> 00:05:28.560
When they submit, they are redirected
to the legitimate site and never know

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.409
they have had their details stolen.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.939
Nicole: Other forms of reverse social
engineering involve posting repeatedly

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:39.500
on a victim’s social media site until
the victim becomes curious and visits the

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.793
bogus site, therefore the link is made.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:45.030
So how can social engineering
attacks be prevented?

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:49.250
Abraham: Threat actors will always be able
to find a way to exploit the vulnerability

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.719
of users but if the appropriate cyber
security measures are in place and

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:58.239
people are educated and informed about
potential threat of attacks through social

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.800
engineering the incidence may reduce.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:05.150
Nicole: We all must remember to be more
vigilant in everything we do especially

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:10.150
online, not randomly put information
out there to be exploited, learn to

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:15.720
not trust so readily, and think more
carefully about what we click on, we might

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.309
lessen our chances of becoming a victim.

